{
  "id": "rotate-list",
  "questionId": "rotate-list",
  "slug": "rotate-list",
  "title": "Rotate List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Linked list",
  "patterns": [
    "linked list",
    "cyclic shift"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "k can be much larger than the list length - what's the actual effective rotation amount?",
    "reduce k modulo the list length first, since rotating by the full length is a no-op",
    "if you connected the tail back to the head to form a cycle, where would you need to 'cut' it to get the rotated list?",
    "the new tail is at index (n - k % n - 1) and the new head is right after it - walk to that point and reassemble, or equivalently slice and concatenate the array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "not reducing k modulo the list length, causing unnecessary work or wrong results when k is huge",
    "off-by-one when computing the split point for the new head/tail",
    "not handling an empty list or a list of length 1, where k has no effect"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order, along with an integer `k`.\n\n**Rotate the list to the right by `k` places, meaning each node moves `k` positions toward the end, with nodes that fall off the end wrapping around to the front, and return the values of the resulting list in order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 500",
      "-1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000",
      "0 <= k <= 2 * 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "an empty list",
      "a single-node list (any k leaves it unchanged)",
      "k larger than the length of the list (k should effectively be reduced modulo the length)",
      "k equal to a multiple of the list's length (no visible change)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2",
        "output": "[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "the last 2 values wrap around to the front"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [0, 1, 2], k = 4",
        "output": "[2, 0, 1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 0, 1]",
        "explanation": "k = 4 is equivalent to k = 1 for a list of length 3, since rotating by the full length changes nothing"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [], k = 0",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": ""
      }
    ],
